  "text": "None of the three proposed answers matches the bound I recall from the literature. I could not check the source here, so this rests on memory.\n\n- **Solution 1 (7t−8):** The upper-bound argument depends on unproven \"reach\" and \"scattering\" lemmas, and the sharpness construction is only sketched. The t=2 case, diameter 6, is a specific claim I can't verify.\n- **Solution 2 (2t−1):** The \"counting argument\" is hand-waved and gives no real proof of the upper bound. Its sharpness claim is also vague.\n- **Solution 3 (⌊(3t−1)/2⌋):** The cycle example checks out for t=2. I verified that f = x₁⋯x₅ is not in NI(C₅)² (degree 5 < 6), and that x₁f lies in NI(C₅)² via N[2]·N[5]. But exhibiting one graph only gives a lower bound on the maximum diameter. The claim that cycles are the only possible extremal graphs is asserted without proof, so it does not establish the upper bound.\n\nMy recollection is that the known result is diam(G) ≤ 3t−1 for graphs with m ∈ Ass(NI(G)ᵗ). None of the three proposed answers equals 3t−1. This recollection is not verified, so treat the conclusion as tentative.\n\nCHOICE: NONE",
